WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.193
Aerobic and resistance exercise challenge a connected neuroendocrine system.

00:00:05.193 --> 00:00:11.497
Neural signals coordinate movement, while hormones help regulate fuel use, stress, and reproduction.

00:00:11.497 --> 00:00:20.889
In this film, we follow serotonin, also called five H T, and progesterone, or P four, from a single exercise bout to long term adaptation.

00:00:20.889 --> 00:00:23.194
The two training modes overlap.

00:00:23.194 --> 00:00:28.187
Duration, intensity, nutrition, and menstrual cycle phase all shape the response.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:36.763
During prolonged aerobic exercise, fat mobilization increases circulating free fatty acids.

00:00:36.763 --> 00:00:41.422
These can displace some tryptophan from albumin, increasing the free fraction.

00:00:41.422 --> 00:00:46.286
Meanwhile, working muscle may take up and oxidize branched chain amino acids.

00:00:46.286 --> 00:00:54.324
A higher ratio of free tryptophan to competing large neutral amino acids can favor tryptophan entry into the brain through the L A T one transporter.

00:00:54.324 --> 00:01:00.242
Tryptophan is a precursor for serotonin, synthesized in neurons including those of the raphe nuclei.

00:01:00.242 --> 00:01:06.640
The central fatigue hypothesis proposes that altered serotonin signaling contributes to fatigue and reduced motor drive.

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:13.323
But this is one part of a complex system involving other neurotransmitters, temperature, motivation, and peripheral fatigue.

00:01:13.323 --> 00:01:17.302
Resistance exercise also produces central and peripheral fatigue.

00:01:17.302 --> 00:01:25.204
Fixed percentage differences between training modes are not universal, and a change in blood amino acids does not directly measure brain serotonin.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:36.233
A blood test reports concentration, not the total amount of hormone in circulation.

00:01:36.233 --> 00:01:43.432
Exercise related fluid shifts can reduce plasma volume, so the same hormone amount produces a higher measured concentration.

00:01:43.432 --> 00:01:47.351
Genuine changes in secretion and clearance may occur at the same time.

00:01:47.351 --> 00:01:53.759
The adrenal glands can contribute progesterone, but the corpus luteum remains a major source during the luteal phase.

00:01:53.759 --> 00:01:59.252
An acute rise should not automatically be called an illusion, or assigned entirely to the adrenals.

00:01:59.252 --> 00:02:07.280
There is no single fifteen to forty five percent rise, or thirty to sixty minute recovery rule, that applies to every athlete and protocol.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:11.623
Interpret samples with cycle phase, hydration, workload, and timing.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.779
Regular exercise can improve mood, sleep, and stress resilience.

00:02:20.779 --> 00:02:24.033
Adaptations in serotonin circuits are complex.

00:02:24.033 --> 00:02:30.336
Specific receptor changes reported in animal studies should not be presented as a universal human training response.

00:02:30.336 --> 00:02:38.063
A separate issue is prolonged low energy availability: too little dietary energy remains after exercise to support other body functions.

00:02:38.063 --> 00:02:46.436
Problematic low energy availability can disrupt hypothalamic signaling and ovulation, lowering luteal progesterone alongside other endocrine changes.

00:02:46.436 --> 00:02:51.094
Relative energy deficiency in sport can impair bone health and increase injury risk.

00:02:51.094 --> 00:02:54.213
This risk is not exclusive to aerobic athletes.

00:02:54.213 --> 00:03:02.801
Resistance and impact loading can stimulate bone adaptation, but lifting does not guarantee reproductive hormone equilibrium or cancel the effects of under fueling.

00:03:02.801 --> 00:03:07.544
Evaluate nutrition, training load, menstrual history, and recovery together.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:24.019
For sustained endurance exercise, thirty to sixty grams of carbohydrate per hour is a common starting range, tailored to duration, intensity, and gut tolerance.

00:03:24.019 --> 00:03:27.051
Carbohydrate supports performance through several mechanisms.

00:03:27.051 --> 00:03:30.307
Adding B C A As is not a proven way to block central fatigue.

00:03:30.307 --> 00:03:34.244
Assess menstrual changes and other signs of under fueling with a qualified clinician.

00:03:34.244 --> 00:03:36.921
Time progesterone testing to the individual cycle.

00:03:36.921 --> 00:03:40.718
A single five nanogram per milliliter value is not a universal health target.

00:03:40.718 --> 00:03:44.000
Restore adequate energy availability with an individualized plan.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:49.594
Explore the references and evidence notes accompanying this film.

00:03:49.594 --> 00:03:54.707
Use mechanisms to guide questions, and the athlete’s context to guide interpretation.